    <DataNode elementId="CPD-4" textLabel="molybdopterin" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="HPEUEJRPDGMIMY-IFQPEPLCSA-L" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="1371.7164306640625" centerY="1310.5587158203125" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="xue, PMN, nilo-poyanco">In all molybdenum-containing enzymes except nitrogenase, the metal is coordinated to the organic cofactor molybdopterin (MPT), which also serves as the cofactor for tungsten-containing enzymes. When molybdenum is present, the compound is referred to as a |FRAME: CPD-15870|. The MPT molecule is a substituted pterin ring that coordinates the metal through a dithiolene linkage. The name H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;Dtpp-mP has been suggested as the systematic name for this compound |CITS: [9861725]|.</Comment>
